<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>language-development</title><style>table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{border:1px solid #333;padding:6px}th{background:#eee}img{max-width:100%}</style></head><body><h1>language-development</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Unit 1 — Education and Humanity</li><li>Unit 2 — Communication</li><li>Unit 3 — Media and Society</li></ul><h2>Unit 1 — Education and Humanity</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>### 1. Meaning of Education</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Education is the systematic process of facilitating learning, acquiring knowledge, skills, values, and habits. It is not merely schooling but a lifelong journey of personal and social development.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>### 2. Types of Education</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>- **Formal Education**: Structured, curriculum-based learning in schools, colleges, and universities.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>- **Non-Formal Education**: Organized learning outside the formal system (adult education, vocational training).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>- **Informal Education**: Lifelong learning from family, peers, media, and life experiences.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>### 3. Education and Humanity</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>True education must nurture humanity — empathy, ethical reasoning, and social responsibility. When education becomes purely job-oriented, it risks producing skilled but morally empty individuals.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>### 4. Goals of Education</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>- Intellectual: Developing critical thinking and creativity</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>- Moral: Building character and ethical judgment</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>- Social: Fostering cooperation and civic responsibility</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Three types: formal (schools, colleges, universities), non-formal (adult education, vocational training), informal (family, peers, media, experience).</td></tr><tr><td>Education is a lifelong journey of personal and social development, not only a classroom activity.</td></tr><tr><td>Intellectual goal develops critical thinking and creativity; moral goal builds character and ethical judgment; social goal fosters cooperation and civic responsibility; economic goal prepares for productive employment.</td></tr><tr><td>Informal learning shapes attitudes and values even where there is no formal schooling.</td></tr><tr><td>Nepal&#x27;s system evolved from gurushekha to modern schooling.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Education = Schooling. ✅ Education is broader — it includes life experiences.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Only formal education matters. ✅ Informal learning shapes attitudes and values.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Unit 1 presents education as a systematic, lifelong process of learning rather than mere schooling. It classifies education into formal, non-formal and informal forms and links each type to everyday examples. The unit then argues that true education must build humanity - empathy, ethical reasoning and social responsibility - instead of producing purely job-ready skills. It closes with the four goals of education and the evolution of the education system in Nepal from gurushekha to modern schooling under the National Education Plan, 2019.</p><hr><h2>Unit 2 — Communication</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>### 1. What is Communication?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Communication is the exchange of information, ideas, feelings, and meaning between two or more people through symbols, signs, or behavior.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>### 2. Elements of Communication</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>- **Sender**: The person who initiates the message</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>- **Message**: The content being communicated</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>- **Channel/Medium**: The method of transmission (verbal, written, digital)</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>- **Receiver**: The person who gets the message</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>- **Feedback**: The response from the receiver</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>- **Noise**: Any interference that distorts the message</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>### 3. Types of Communication</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>- **Verbal**: Spoken or written words</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>- **Non-verbal**: Body language, gestures, facial expressions</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Six elements in order: sender, message, channel or medium, receiver, feedback and noise.</td></tr><tr><td>The channel or medium is the method of transmission (verbal, written, digital), not the person who sends the message.</td></tr><tr><td>Non-verbal communication covers body language, gestures and facial expressions.</td></tr><tr><td>Four barriers: physical (distance, noise), psychological (prejudice, emotion), semantic (language differences), cultural (norms, values).</td></tr><tr><td>The 5 Cs of effective communication are clarity, conciseness, correctness, courtesy and confidence.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Communication is only talking. ✅ It includes listening, observing, and interpreting.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ More words = better communication. ✅ Clarity and conciseness matter more.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Unit 2 defines communication as the exchange of information, ideas, feelings and meaning between two or more people through symbols, signs or behaviour. It sets out the six elements - sender, message, channel, receiver, feedback and noise - and the types, including verbal, non-verbal, visual and active listening. The unit then groups the barriers into physical, psychological, semantic and cultural, and closes with the 5 Cs of effective communication: clarity, conciseness, correctness, courtesy and confidence.</p><hr><h2>Unit 3 — Media and Society</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>### 1. Media: Definition and Types</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Media refers to the channels through which news, entertainment, education, and opinion are delivered. Types: Print (newspapers, magazines), Broadcast (TV, radio), Digital (social media, websites).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>### 2. Role of Media in Society</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>- Informing the public about current events</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>- Educating and raising awareness</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>- Providing entertainment</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>- Acting as a watchdog over government</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>- Shaping public opinion and culture</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>### 3. Media Ethics</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Accuracy, fairness, independence, accountability, and minimizing harm are core principles of media ethics.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>### 4. Impact of Social Media</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Social media has democratized information but also spread misinformation. Critical media literacy is essential.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Definition: media are the channels through which news, entertainment, education and opinion are delivered.</td></tr><tr><td>Three types: print (newspapers, magazines), broadcast (TV, radio), digital (social media, websites).</td></tr><tr><td>Five functions: inform the public about current events, educate and raise awareness, entertain, act as a watchdog over government, and shape public opinion and culture.</td></tr><tr><td>Five ethical principles: accuracy, fairness, independence, accountability and minimizing harm.</td></tr><tr><td>Social media democratized information but also spread misinformation, so critical media literacy is essential.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Media only entertains. ✅ Media informs, educates, and holds power accountable.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Social media is always reliable. ✅ Verification and media literacy are crucial.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Unit 3 defines media as the channels through which news, entertainment, education and opinion are delivered, and groups them into print, broadcast and digital. It examines the media&#x27;s roles in society - informing the public, educating, entertaining, acting as a watchdog over government, and shaping public opinion and culture - together with the ethics of accuracy, fairness, independence, accountability and minimizing harm. The unit then weighs the double-edged impact of social media, stresses the need for critical media literacy, and refers to the Nepali press, where radio and TV remain dominant in rural areas.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Unit 1 — Education and Humanity</td><td>Unit 1 presents education as a systematic, lifelong process of learning rather than mere schooling. It classifies education into formal, non-formal and informal forms and links each type to everyday examples. The unit th</td></tr><tr><td>2: Unit 2 — Communication</td><td>Unit 2 defines communication as the exchange of information, ideas, feelings and meaning between two or more people through symbols, signs or behaviour. It sets out the six elements - sender, message, channel, receiver, </td></tr><tr><td>3: Unit 3 — Media and Society</td><td>Unit 3 defines media as the channels through which news, entertainment, education and opinion are delivered, and groups them into print, broadcast and digital. It examines the media&#x27;s roles in society - informing the pub</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Language Development.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>Close reading builds an interpretation from precise details—word choice, syntax, imagery, sound, form and structure. A label such as ‘metaphor’ or ‘alliteration’ is only a starting observation; explain its effect in this particular passage.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>Support claims with accurate, located evidence and distinguish observation from interpretation. When two readings fit, explain evidence for each and acknowledge counterevidence; do not state authorial intention as fact without a source.</p><h3>Open research question — How evidence supports more than one reading</h3><p>Literary interpretation is evidence-based rather than a hunt for one hidden answer. A defensible open reading offers at least two text-supported interpretations, cites exact details, and explains what complicates each; authorial intention should not be asserted without evidence.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>In “Language Development”, which two interpretations can the exact wording support, and what detail complicates each? Record the line or passage; leave the issue open if the text does not decide it.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://www.york.ac.uk/english/about/writing-at-york/writing-resources/close-reading/">University of York: close reading and ambiguity</a></li><li><a href="https://writing.wisc.edu/handbook/academicwriting/closereading/">UW–Madison: close reading</a></li><li><a href="https://owl.purdue.edu/owl/general_writing/academic_writing/rhetorical_situation/elements_of_rhetorical_situations.html">Purdue OWL: rhetorical situation</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>